sentences.sentence_text:
5
The Tao doesn't take sides;
it gives birth to both good and evil.

Be like the forces of nature:
when it blows, there is only wind;
when it rains, there is only rain;
when the clouds pass, the sun shines through.

Open yourself to the Tao,
then trust your natural responses;
and everything will fall into place.

There is a time for being ahead,
a time for being behind;
a time for being in motion,
a time for being at rest;
a time for being vigorous,
a time for being exhausted;
a time for being safe,
a time for being in danger.

31
Weapons are the tools of violence;
all decent men detest them.

33
Knowing others is intelligence;
knowing yourself is true wisdom.

38
The Master doesn't try to be powerful;
thus he is truly powerful.

Because, being one with the Tao,
when you seek, you find;
and when you make a mistake, you are forgiven.

63
Act without doing;
work without effort.

76
Men are born soft and supple;
dead, they are stiff and hard.

81
True words aren't eloquent;
eloquent words aren't true.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::13:
5
The Tao doesn't take sides;
it gives birth to both good and evil. The Master doesn't take sides;
she welcomes both saints and sinners.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::14:
The Tao is like a bellows:
it is empty yet infinitely capable. The more you use it, the more it produces;
the more you talk of it, the less you understand.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::18:
7
The Tao is infinite, eternal. Why is it eternal? It was never born;
thus it can never die. Why is it infinite? It has no desires for itself;
thus it is present for all beings.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::19:
The Master stays behind;
that is why she is ahead. She is detached from all things;
that is why she is one with them. Because she has let go of herself,
she is perfectly fulfilled.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::40:
15
The ancient Masters were profound and subtle. Their wisdom was unfathomable. There is no way to describe it;
all we can describe is their appearance.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::55:
Other people have what they need;
I alone possess nothing. I alone drift about,
like someone without a home. I am like an idiot, my mind is so empty.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::56:
Other people are bright;
I alone am dark. Other people are sharper;
I alone am dull. Other people have a purpose;
I alone don't know. I drift like a wave on the ocean,
I blow as aimless as the wind.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::62:
22
If you want to become whole,
let yourself be partial. If you want to become straight,
let yourself be crooked. If you want to become full,
let yourself be empty. If you want to be reborn,
let yourself die. If you want to be given everything,
give everything up.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::65:
23
Express yourself completely,
then keep quiet. Be like the forces of nature:
when it blows, there is only wind;
when it rains, there is only rain;
when the clouds pass, the sun shines through.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::67:
Open yourself to the Tao,
then trust your natural responses;
and everything will fall into place.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::86:
There is a time for being ahead,
a time for being behind;
a time for being in motion,
a time for being at rest;
a time for being vigorous,
a time for being exhausted;
a time for being safe,
a time for being in danger.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::90:
31
Weapons are the tools of violence;
all decent men detest them.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::97:
33
Knowing others is intelligence;
knowing yourself is true wisdom. Mastering others is strength;
mastering yourself is true power.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::108:
38
The Master doesn't try to be powerful;
thus he is truly powerful. The ordinary man keeps reaching for power;
thus he never has enough.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::128:
Be content with what you have;
rejoice in the way things are. When you realize there is nothing lacking,
the whole world belongs to you.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::147:
52
In the beginning was the Tao. All things issue from it;
all things return to it.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::157:
The Master's power is like this. He lets all things come and go
effortlessly, without desire. He never expects results;
thus he is never disappointed. He is never disappointed;
thus his spirit never grows old.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::179:
Why did the ancient Masters esteem the Tao? Because, being one with the Tao,
when you seek, you find;
and when you make a mistake, you are forgiven. That is why everybody loves it.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::181:
The Master never reaches for the great;
thus she achieves greatness. When she runs into a difficulty,
she stops and gives herself to it. She doesn't cling to her own comfort;
thus problems are no problem for her.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::203:
71
Not-knowing is true knowledge. Presuming to know is a disease. First realize that you are sick;
then you can move toward health.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::213:
76
Men are born soft and supple;
dead, they are stiff and hard. Plants are born tender and pliant;
dead, they are brittle and dry.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::220:
The soft overcomes the hard;
the gentle overcomes the rigid. Everyone knows this is true,
but few can put it into practice.

paragraph_cache.doc_2_Tao_Te_Ching__Lao_Tzu::226:
81
True words aren't eloquent;
eloquent words aren't true. Wise men don't need to prove their point;
men who need to prove their point aren't wise.
